telnetd: store a session's output into the network buffer directly

The loop that carries what a session prints from the pty to the socket
hands every byte to output_data("%c"), which formats it with
vsnprintf() and sends the buffer to the socket once it is three-quarters
full: a printf for each character, and two short writes for each read
of the pty.  The loop checks that the buffer has room for what one pass
adds before it makes the pass, so the bytes go in as they are, and the
buffer leaves in one write when the main loop finds the socket ready.

sw/fasttelnetd/Makefile applies this to a copy of telnetd.c; the file
in the NetBSD tree stays as imported.

--- a/libexec/telnetd/telnetd.c
+++ b/libexec/telnetd/telnetd.c
@@ -1074,14 +1074,14 @@
 				break;
 			c = *ptyip++ & 0377, pcc--;
 			if (c == IAC)
-				output_data("%c", c);
-			output_data("%c", c);
+				*nfrontp++ = c;
+			*nfrontp++ = c;
 			if ((c == '\r') && (my_state_is_wont(TELOPT_BINARY))) {
 				if (pcc > 0 && ((*ptyip & 0377) == '\n')) {
-					output_data("%c", *ptyip++ & 0377);
+					*nfrontp++ = *ptyip++ & 0377;
 					pcc--;
 				} else
-					output_datalen("\0", 1);
+					*nfrontp++ = '\0';
 			}
 		}
 
